Now reader.rs.

Careful with `self.header()` borrow: `header()` takes &mut self returns SegmentHeader (Copy) — fine.

Implement:

```rust
pub struct SegmentReader<'a> {
    buf: &'a [u8],
    pos: usize,
    last_seq: Option<u64>,
    status: ReaderStatus,
    header: Option<SegmentHeader>,
    stopped: bool,
    records: u64,
    last_valid_offset: usize,
}
```

new: pos = 0 (header not parsed), status Clean.

header(&mut self) as planned.

next_record loop:
```rust
pub fn next_record(&mut self) -> Option<Record<'a>> {
    if self.stopped { return None; }
    loop {
        let header = match self.header() { Ok(h) => h, Err(_) => return None };
        let remaining = self.buf.len() - self.pos;
        if remaining == 0 { self.stopped = true; return None; }
        if remaining < RECORD_HEADER_LEN {
            self.status = ReaderStatus::Torn { at: self.pos };
            self.stopped = true;
            return None;
        }
        let expected = Some(match self.last_seq { Some(seq) => seq + 1, None => header.seq_first });
        match self.parse_at(self.pos, expected) {
            Ok(record) => { ...; return Some(record); }
            Err(_) => {
                match self.resync() {
                    Some(next) => { /* continue loop */ }
                    None => { self.stopped = true; return None; }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Careful: `self.pos = next` then loop → header() returns cached, remaining computed, parse at pos with expected seq (from last_seq; resync already validated seq >= min_seq, but exact expected might not match, e.g. after skipping one damaged record seq jumps by 2 → primary parse would fail with UnexpectedSeq → resync again from pos+1 → this time pos is the valid record, so resync scan starts at pos+1 and skips it! Infinite/wrong.

Fix: after resync finds a valid record at `next`, parse and accept it directly in the loop (don't re-validate expected seq). Implement resync returning `Option<Record<'a>>` plus setting pos; or handle in loop:

```rust
Err(_) => {
    if let Some(record) = self.resync() {
        // resync already validated the record fully (magic, len, crc, seq >= min)
        self.accept(&record);
        return Some(record);
    }
    self.stopped = true;
    return None;
}
```
where `resync(&mut self) -> Option<Record<'a>>` finds and returns the record at found offset, and sets self.pos to that offset (before accept sets pos to end). Let's make `accept` set last_seq, last_valid_offset, pos, records.

In resync, when found: set status Damaged { at: start } if currently Clean; return Ok(record). But careful: resync's record is constructed via `parse_record_at(self.buf, candidate)?` (no expected seq), then check seq >= min_seq. Return it.

Then accept(&record) sets self.pos = candidate + record.record_len().

Implement:

```rust
fn resync(&mut self) -> Option<Record<'a>> {
    let start = self.pos;
    let min_seq = match self.last_seq {
        Some(seq) => seq + 1,
        None => self.header.map_or(0, |h| h.seq_first),
    };
    let mut candidate = start + 1;
    while candidate + RECORD_HEADER_LEN <= self.buf.len() {
        if self.buf[candidate..candidate + 4] == RECORD_MAGIC_BYTES {
            if let Ok(record) = parse_record_at(self.buf, candidate) {
                if record.header.seq >= min_seq {
                    if self.status == ReaderStatus::Clean {
                        self.status = ReaderStatus::Damaged { at: start };
                    }
                    return Some(record);
                }
            }
        }
        candidate += 1;
    }
    let magic_present = start + 4 <= self.buf.len()
        && self.buf[start..start + 4] == RECORD_MAGIC_BYTES;
    self.status = if magic_present {
        ReaderStatus::Torn { at: start }
    } else {
        ReaderStatus::Damaged { at: start }
    };
    None
}
```
Hmm: when status already Damaged and we resync again later, keep Damaged (first). `if self.status == Clean` — good.

Wait, there's an issue with `self.header` field: header() fills it. In resync I access self.header — fine.

Also, `parse_record_at` borrow: it returns Record<'a> borrowed from self.buf — but `self.buf` is `&'a [u8]`; calling `parse_record_at(self.buf, candidate)` — the return type lifetime: function signature `parse_record_at(bytes: &[u8], offset) -> Result<Record<'_>, Error>` elides to lifetime of `bytes` argument, which is `self.buf` (borrowed from self with shorter lifetime? `self.buf` copies the reference; its type is &'a [u8] so the returned record is Record<'a>). Actually `&[u8]` elision: `parse_record_at<'b>(bytes: &'b [u8], ...) -> Result<Record<'b>, _>`. Passing `self.buf` which is `&'a [u8]` value → 'b can be 'a (reborrow of the copy is 'a; since it's a copy of the reference, not a borrow of self). Rust will coerce to the longer 'a? When passing `self.buf: &'a [u8]` by value, 'b = 'a. ✓ But careful: if the compiler reborrows as `&'x [u8]` where 'x is the borrow of self... since the value is Copy, no reborrow occurs. Should be fine.

